vice door). Careless scripts (kind careless) do the same violence seen by a camera, seen by a crowd, found before the exit, or reported unstopped, each against its careful twin (MC-7). Trail is compared in total, the mission's plus what the debrief carries into the campaign. Rev 4 (2026-10-16): a found body holds the doors in its sight (careless-guard-left-door-held); open cases are priced at the rulebook's case-trail-eq in the totals. The driver route (Motive §6.2) is still not scripted: with the pickup loop and three bodies to a service door it plays, but each staff victim's missed mark is its own incident (the second lands at Alert 2, and the search opens the service door), and every witness at the bay tells a Loyal workmate by telephone (briefs/013/waymark-handover.md §5). Brief 018 (Waymark, 2026-10-18; Keel's engine 0.7.0, the getaway): extract is retired into Board and Go. Each quiet route has two references: fight it out (the original id: the Enforcer waits for the crew to break, boards, and the car leaves; the three walk out) and board and go (<id>-board, the primary of A, B and G: the Enforcer walks Vale to the car as the ambush springs, the three board once the blocker is down, nobody left behind). Loud has board and go (loud), fight it out (loud-fight) and the cheapest (loud-fast), each boarding the whole cell, since the seal clock captures anyone left standing. loud-left-downed and loud-left-standing are the two runs that leave an op behind (captured downed; captured fighting, abandoned). Plans may wait for the engine's `blocker.out` (c019: the engine logs it, and `car.free`). c019 (engine 0.7.x: walking Vale, the Enforcer does not fire): plans recomposed; loud-fast keeps its cover and carries its downed Fixer in. MC-10 checks quiet beats loud on Trail (a captive's +4 T counts as campaign Trail), Alert, Standing and the seal; MC-11 that no ambush spot or police stand holds the car.",
